"use strict";(self.webpackChunkwebsite=self.webpackChunkwebsite||[]).push([["5823"],{3032(e,t,r){r.r(t),r.d(t,{metadata:()=>o,default:()=>p,frontMatter:()=>i,contentTitle:()=>s,toc:()=>d,assets:()=>l});var o=JSON.parse('{"id":"reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport","title":"How to Work With and Create Coverage Map Reports","description":"alms \xb7 imported from live Hugo on 2026-10-07. Guides stay latest. Original page.","source":"@site/docs/reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport.md","sourceDirName":"reference/reports/howtos","slug":"/reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport","permalink":"/personal-av-docs/docs/reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport","draft":false,"unlisted":false,"editUrl":"https://github.com/andreyjv/personal-av/tree/docs/documentation-revamp/PRD_documentation-revamp/website/docs/reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport.md","tags":[],"version":"current","sidebarPosition":99,"frontMatter":{"title":"How to Work With and Create Coverage Map Reports","sidebar_label":"How to Work With and Create Coverage Map","sidebar_position":99,"edition":"alms","source":"https://doc.airvantage.net/alms/reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport","format":"md"}}'),n=r(4848),a=r(8453);let i={title:"How to Work With and Create Coverage Map Reports",sidebar_label:"How to Work With and Create Coverage Map",sidebar_position:99,edition:"alms",source:"https://doc.airvantage.net/alms/reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport",format:"md"},s,l={},d=[];function c(e){let t={a:"a",p:"p",strong:"strong",...(0,a.R)(),...e.components};return(0,n.jsxs)(n.Fragment,{children:[(0,n.jsxs)(t.p,{children:[":::note Edition\n",(0,n.jsx)(t.strong,{children:"alms"})," \xb7 imported from live Hugo on 2026-10-07. Guides stay latest. ",(0,n.jsx)(t.a,{href:"https://doc.airvantage.net/alms/reference/reports/howtos/createcoveragemapreport",children:"Original page"}),".\n:::"]}),"\n",(0,n.jsx)(t.p,{children:"How to Work With and Create Coverage Map Reports\nThe Coverage Map report shows you system network connectivity status during trips within the reporting period. The Coverage Map consists of squares shaded green (good), amber (average) or red (poor) based on the percentage of cellular uptime during the trip segment. You can use this report to identify regions that may have connectivity quality-of-service issues.\nBy default, locations where the system spent less than 30 seconds are not shown.\nYou can view results for various radios, carriers, cellular technologies and radio bands by selecting from the lists at the top of the map.\nRadios\nrefers to the cellular radio used by the router. The list may include:\nCellular\n: Internal radio module for a single-radio router\nXP Cellular\n: Radio module in the optional XP cartridge for an AirLink XR80\nXP1 Cellular\n: Radio module in the XP1 cartridge for an AirLink XR90\nXP2 Cellular\n: Radio module in the XP2 cartridge for an AirLink XR90\nLPWA Cellular\n: Internal radio module used for ALMS Out of Band Management\nHPUE Modem\n: External modem connected to a router Ethernet port\nSingle-radio routers include the AirLink RV55, RX55, LX60, MP70 and XR80 (base model). Multi-radio routers include the AirLink XR80 (with XP cartridge) and XR90.\nTo view the details for a map segment, click the desired segment.\nSamples\nis the number of all WAN-related datapoints reported by the system(s) during the report period.\nCreating a Coverage Map Report\nFrom the Reporting dashboard, click the\nCreate a New Report\nicon.\nAn empty report screen appears.\nSelect a Template\nChoose how often you\u2019d like this report to be run.\nClick:\nSingle\nfor a one-time report\nWeekly\nMonthly\nYearly\nUnder\nReport Properties\n, you can complete the scheduling for your new report. The section below describes how to configure the time and the day on which you\u2019d like your report to run.\nTo run a report immediately, click\nRun Report\n.\nDefine the Report Properties\nThe required fields for creating a valid report are\nType\n,\nName\nand\nRange\n.\nSelect the Report Type\nSelect\nCoverage Map\nfrom the\nType\nmenu.\nEnter a Name and Description\nGive your report a brief, descriptive name. You may want to use a driver\u2019s name, a vehicle model, and/or the AirLink gateway or router model, depending on the type of report you run.\nBelow the\nName\nfield, you can enter a longer report description. You may want to include the report\u2019s schedule and reporting zone.\nSelect the Reporting Range\nThe Range menu lists the options for the reporting period. You can select a predefined Reporting Range, or configure a Custom range.\nThe maximum report duration for Coverage Map is 31 days.\nIf scheduling a recurring report, select one of the relative date ranges (\nYesterday\nor\nLast 7 Days\n, for  example). Selecting an absolute range will generate the same results each time.\nIf you click\nCustom\n, a calendar appears, allowing you to select a start date/time and an end date/time for your report.\nSelect Recipient Email Addresses\nClick the\nRecipient Emails\nfield to select the people you would like to be notified when the report is run.\nYou can also manually enter email addresses in this field.\nSelect the Export File Formats\nCoverage Map reports can be exported as .kml files.\nTo select the export file format, click the\nKML\nbutton.\nSelect Coverage Quality Intervals\nUse the sliders to adjust the percentages for reporting Poor, Average and Good coverage.\nSelect the Systems\nSelect the systems for which you want to run your new report. Select checkboxes for the desired system(s), or filter the list to narrow down your choices. You do not need to use the checkboxes to select specific systems. Selecting the checkboxes will override general filters.\nYou can include a maximum of 100 systems in a Coverage Map report.\nSelect Advanced Settings: Start and End Dates\nThe Advanced settings are optional. If you enable these settings, you may want to set a reminder (external to AirVantage) to notify yourself of the start and end dates.\nClick the\nAdvanced\nbutton to display options for selecting start and end dates for your reports. For example, you can configure a weekly report to run for two months starting one week from today.\nClicking Start Time or Run Until displays a calendar on which you can define a start date/time and end date/time."})]})}function p(e={}){let{wrapper:t}={...(0,a.R)(),...e.components};return t?(0,n.jsx)(t,{...e,children:(0,n.jsx)(c,{...e})}):c(e)}},8453(e,t,r){r.d(t,{R:()=>i,x:()=>s});var o=r(6540);let n={},a=o.createContext(n);function i(e){let t=o.useContext(a);return o.useMemo(function(){return"function"==typeof e?e(t):{...t,...e}},[t,e])}function s(e){let t;return t=e.disableParentContext?"function"==typeof e.components?e.components(n):e.components||n:i(e.components),o.createElement(a.Provider,{value:t},e.children)}}}]);